{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VwaWfbrBE4zs4dsxHEJhfb","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T122440/median/paper.tex has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0fb7cd10-4709-4959-b132-f8932334e111","uuid":"5d87c51f-9066-401b-be31-e5c34288879d","timestamp":"2026-05-21T13:02:54.293Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T122440/median/paper.tex","oldString":"\\bibitem[Zhuang et~al.(2024)]{zhuang2024efficient}\nZhuang, Y., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock Efficient LLM evaluation via IRT.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2401.06599}.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}","newString":"\\bibitem[Zhang et~al.(2024)]{zhang2024gsm1k}\nZhang, H., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock GSM1K: A verifiable math benchmark for evaluating LLM reasoning.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2405.00332}.\n\n\\bibitem[Zhuang et~al.(2024)]{zhuang2024efficient}\nZhuang, Y., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock Efficient LLM evaluation via IRT.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2401.06599}.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}","originalFile":"\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage{times}\n\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{cleveref}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\n\\hypersetup{colorlinks=true,linkcolor=blue!60!black,citecolor=blue!60!black,urlcolor=blue!60!black}\n\n\\title{Psychometric DIF Analysis Reveals 25--29\\% Temporal Item Instability\\\\in MMLU: A Diagnostic Lens for Benchmark Maintenance}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n%% ======================================================================\n%% ABSTRACT\n%% ======================================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\nLarge Language Model (LLM) benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability---that scores measured today and a year ago reflect the same latent construct.\nWe provide a practical \\textbf{psychometric audit protocol} that tests this assumption using Mantel--Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis, applied to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items).\nWe find that approximately 25--29\\% of MMLU items exhibit large DIF (ETS Category C) between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts, after controlling for ability confounds (permutation null: 4.8\\%, matched-ability: 28.9\\%).\nThis instability is predominantly item-specific: domain-level accuracy improvement explains at most 2.4 percentage points (pp) of the 31.3\\% raw rate (pseudo $R^2$=0.0007).\nCrucially, instability does not threaten ranking validity ($\\rho\\!\\approx\\!0.997$ after removing all flagged items), but it reveals which capability dimensions shift across model generations.\nWe further show that the field's standard web-overlap ground truth cannot evaluate item-level differential detectors (enrichment$\\approx$1.0), and propose a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates false positive rate (FPR) inflation ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000).\nWe release a DIF audit protocol for benchmark maintainers specifying monitoring frequency, decision rules, and response options.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%% ======================================================================\n%% 1. INTRODUCTION\n%% ======================================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n\nWhen we claim ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we implicitly assume that both scores measure the same latent construct.\nIn psychometrics, this assumption is formally tested through Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis, which detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups \\emph{after controlling for overall ability} \\citep{holland1993differential}.\nIf a substantial fraction of benchmark items function differently for models released in different time periods, cross-temporal score comparisons lose their psychometric foundation.\n\nWe apply the Mantel--Haenszel (MH) DIF procedure---the most widely used method in educational testing---to the METABENCH response matrix \\citep{perlitz2024metabench}, comprising 5,227 LLMs evaluated on 12,508 MMLU items.\nThis constitutes, to our knowledge, the largest DIF analysis ever conducted on an LLM benchmark.\n\n\\paragraph{Instability $\\neq$ invalidity.}\nOur analysis reveals that approximately 25--29\\% of MMLU items are functionally unstable across model generations (after confound controls).\nHowever, this does not mean MMLU rankings are invalid: the benchmark exhibits massive measurement redundancy, with ranking correlation $\\rho\\!\\approx\\!0.997$ between full and stable-item-only rankings.\nThe diagnostic value of DIF lies not in identifying ``bad items'' for removal, but in revealing \\emph{which} capability dimensions drive ranking shifts across generations.\nThis framing---DIF as diagnostic lens rather than quality filter---is central to our contribution.\n\nOur contributions are:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{Temporal instability characterization}: $\\sim$25--29\\% of MMLU items show large DIF between 2023/2024 model cohorts, robust across thresholds and not driven by aggregate capability improvement ($\\leq$7.6\\% explained).\n    \\item \\textbf{Ground truth meta-critique}: Web-overlap contamination labels measure global exposure, not differential contamination. We provide the first empirical demonstration that enrichment$\\approx$1.0 between DIF flags and web-overlap labels, with semi-synthetic validation confirming DIF detects differential signals ($\\Delta$TPR$=+$0.41 at exploitation rate $p$=0.5).\n    \\item \\textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework}: A reusable $\\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000) for validating any item-level contamination detector.\n    \\item \\textbf{Diagnostic lens and audit protocol}: DIF items carry genuine cross-cohort signal ($z$=$-8$ to $-49\\sigma$ vs.\\ random removal) yet are the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random removal strategy ($z$=$-9.5$ vs.\\ accuracy-gap $z$=$-157$). We provide a concrete audit protocol for benchmark maintainers.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n%% ======================================================================\n%% 2. RELATED WORK\n%% ======================================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.}\nPrior work on detecting benchmark contamination in LLMs falls into several categories: (1)~per-model methods based on memorization signals, including $n$-gram overlap \\citep{dodge2021documenting}, Min-K\\% likelihood analysis \\citep{shi2024detecting}, and perturbation testing \\citep{oren2024proving}; (2)~item-level methods using generation variance \\citep{xu2024benchmarking}; and (3)~metadata-based approaches using web-overlap labels \\citep{li2024task}.\nAll these methods share a common validation limitation: they validate against web-overlap or $n$-gram ground truth that measures \\emph{global exposure} rather than \\emph{differential contamination} at the item level.\n\n\\paragraph{Psychometric methods for LLM evaluation.}\nItem Response Theory (IRT) has been applied to LLM evaluation for adaptive testing \\citep{polo2024tinybenchmarks}, benchmark compression \\citep{zhuang2024efficient}, and model ranking \\citep{belem2024permuteeval}.\nHowever, DIF analysis---a cornerstone of test fairness in educational measurement---remains largely unexplored for LLM benchmarks.\n\\citet{perlitz2024metabench} provide the response matrix infrastructure but do not perform DIF analysis.\n\n\\paragraph{Temporal stability of benchmarks.}\nConcerns about benchmark validity over time have been raised by several studies \\citep{recht2019imagenet,schaeffer2023emergent}, including work on ``growing pains'' in benchmark reuse \\citep{dehghani2021benchmark}.\nOur work provides the first item-level quantification of temporal instability using established psychometric methodology.\n\n%% ======================================================================\n%% 3. METHOD\n%% ======================================================================\n\\section{Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n\n\\subsection{Mantel--Haenszel DIF Analysis}\n\\label{sec:mh-dif}\n\nWe adopt the MH-DIF procedure with the ETS delta classification system \\citep{holland1993differential}.\nModels are divided into \\emph{reference} (pre-2024, $N_{\\text{ref}}\\!=\\!1{,}080$) and \\emph{focal} (2024-H1, $N_{\\text{foc}}\\!=\\!807$) cohorts based on release date.\nFor each item $i$, we compute the MH odds ratio $\\alpha_{\\text{MH}}$ across $K$ strata formed by total-score conditioning, then convert to the ETS delta scale:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\Delta_{\\text{MH}} = -2.35 \\ln(\\hat{\\alpha}_{\\text{MH}})\n\\end{equation}\nItems are classified as ETS A ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}|<1.0$), B ($1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| < 1.5$ and significant), or C ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$ and significant).\nCategory C items exhibit ``large'' DIF by educational testing standards.\n\n\\paragraph{Matching variable.}\nStandard MH conditions on total score within the tested domain.\nFor multi-domain benchmarks like MMLU (57 subjects), within-subject matching risks contamination of the conditioning variable itself (see \\S\\ref{sec:semi-synthetic}).\nWe use cross-subject total score as the primary matching variable.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n\\label{sec:semi-synthetic}\n\nTo evaluate DIF detection power under controlled conditions, we inject known ``contamination'' into the real METABENCH response matrix.\nFor $n_{\\text{inj}}$ items and a subset of focal-cohort models at exploitation rate $p$, we flip responses from incorrect to correct, simulating differential familiarity.\nWe measure $\\Delta$TPR~$=$~TPR$(p)$~$-$~TPR$(0)$ to isolate the detection signal attributable to the injection, removing baseline DIF.\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-subject matching.}\nA key methodological contribution: when injecting contamination into subject $s$, using total score on $s$ as the MH matching variable contaminates the conditioning itself (the injected correct responses inflate the matching score).\nUsing total score on \\emph{other} subjects as the matching variable eliminates this artifact entirely ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000 across 5 tested subjects).\n\n\\subsection{Permutation Null Distribution}\n\\label{sec:permutation}\n\nTo calibrate the observed C\\% against chance, we conduct 1{,}000 permutation tests: randomly reassigning temporal labels (maintaining $N_{\\text{ref}}\\!=\\!1{,}080$, $N_{\\text{foc}}\\!=\\!807$) and recomputing MH-DIF each time.\nThis yields a null distribution for C\\% under the hypothesis that temporal labels carry no DIF-relevant information.\n\n%% ======================================================================\n%% 4. EXPERIMENTS\n%% ======================================================================\n\\section{Experiments}\n\\label{sec:experiments}\n\nAll experiments use the METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 items) unless otherwise noted.\n\n\\subsection{Temporal DIF on MMLU}\n\\label{sec:temporal-dif}\n\n\\Cref{tab:temporal-dif} presents the main DIF results across three cohort definitions.\nThe temporal split (2023 vs.\\ 2024-H1) yields C\\%$=$31.3\\%, while the ability-quintile split and random-split sanity check produce 3.1\\% and 0.21\\% respectively.\nThe 150$\\times$ enrichment ratio (temporal/random) confirms the signal is genuine.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Temporal DIF summary by cohort definition. C\\% = fraction of items classified ETS Category C ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}|\\geq 1.5$, significant). FPR$_{\\text{Li}}$ = fraction of Li et al.\\ web-overlap ``clean'' items flagged. Enrichment = C\\% among web-overlap items / overall C\\%.}\n\\label{tab:temporal-dif}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrr}\n\\toprule\nCohort & $N_{\\text{ref}}$ & $N_{\\text{foc}}$ & Items & C\\% & FPR$_{\\text{Li}}$ & Enrich. \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal (2023/2024) & 1,080 & 807 & 12,508 & 31.3 & 0.322 & 0.964 \\\\\nAbility quintile     & 2,610 & 2,609 & 12,508 & 3.1  & 0.029 & 1.15  \\\\\nRandom split (sanity)& 950   & 950   & 12,508 & 0.21 & 0.002 & 1.01  \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Calibrating against chance.}\n\\Cref{tab:permutation} presents the permutation null distribution alongside three baseline comparisons.\nThe null C\\% mean is 4.8\\% (95\\% CI: [2.7, 7.3]), indicating that temporal labels carry a weak structural signal---model release date and ability are not perfectly orthogonal ($d\\!=\\!-0.17$).\nHowever, the observed 31.3\\% is 6.5$\\times$ the null mean ($z\\!=\\!22.1$, $p < 10^{-50}$), confirming that the vast majority of temporal DIF reflects genuine item-level instability.\nAfter subtracting the null baseline, the estimated genuine DIF rate is $\\sim$26.5\\%.\nCombined with the matched-ability analysis (C\\%$=$28.9\\% after 1:1 nearest-neighbor matching on ability, $d\\!\\approx\\!-0.01$), we estimate \\textbf{$\\sim$25--29\\% of MMLU items are genuinely unstable}, depending on the confound control applied.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Three-baseline comparison for C\\% calibration. The permutation null quantifies the structural signal from temporal labels; matched-ability controls for cohort ability differences.}\n\\label{tab:permutation}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrl}\n\\toprule\nBaseline & C\\% & Interpretation \\\\\n\\midrule\nRandom split (within-cohort)     & 0.18 & Statistical noise floor \\\\\nPermutation null (label shuffle) & 4.8  & Temporal label structure \\\\\nObserved temporal DIF            & 31.3 & Genuine + structural \\\\\nMatched-ability (1:1 NN)         & 28.9 & Genuine after ability control \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Domain specificity.}\nAll 57 MMLU subjects exhibit instability (C\\% range: 20.1--47.8\\%, unweighted mean: 30.4\\%).\nDomain-level accuracy improvement shows a weak positive correlation with DIF rate ($\\rho\\!=\\!0.32$, $p\\!=\\!0.016$), but this ecological correlation is misleading.\n\n\\subsection{Confound Analysis}\n\\label{sec:confound}\n\n\\paragraph{Domain improvement decomposition.}\nLogistic regression predicting DIF-C status from item-level covariates reveals that domain accuracy change explains negligible variance (\\Cref{tab:regression}).\nModel~1 (subject accuracy $\\Delta$ only) yields McFadden $R^2\\!=\\!0.0007$; even the best model (Model~4: item accuracy $\\Delta$ + CTT properties) explains only 4.5\\% of DIF variance.\nThis means $>$95\\% of the item-level DIF signal cannot be attributed to observed covariates---the instability is predominantly item-specific.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Logistic regression predicting DIF-C status. McFadden $R^2$ indicates explained variance. Models 2 and 4 include classical test theory (CTT) item properties (difficulty, discrimination).}\n\\label{tab:regression}\n\\begin{tabular}{llcc}\n\\toprule\nModel & Predictors & McFadden $R^2$ & Nagelkerke $R^2$ \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & Subject acc $\\Delta$ & 0.0007 & 0.0013 \\\\\n2 & Subject acc $\\Delta$ + CTT & 0.044 & 0.074 \\\\\n3 & Item acc $\\Delta$ & 0.0013 & 0.0022 \\\\\n4 & Item acc $\\Delta$ + CTT & 0.045 & 0.076 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Simpson's paradox in accuracy effects.}\nA notable pattern emerges when comparing Models~1 and~2: the odds ratio for subject accuracy $\\Delta$ reverses from 1.58 (positive: higher $\\Delta$ $\\to$ more DIF) in the marginal model to 0.71 (negative: higher $\\Delta$ $\\to$ less DIF) after conditioning on item properties.\nThis occurs because high-discrimination items (OR$=$1.54) are simultaneously DIF-prone \\emph{and} high-accuracy-change items, creating a confound.\nWe report both models without privileging a causal interpretation: Model~1 answers ``does aggregate improvement associate with DIF?'' while Model~2 answers ``does improvement associate with DIF \\emph{beyond what item properties predict}?''\n\n\\paragraph{Architecture homogeneity.}\nMETABENCH MMLU is 99.98\\% decoder-only (5,226/5,227 models).\nExcluding the single non-decoder model changes nothing: C\\%$=$31.3\\%, Jaccard$=$1.0 vs.\\ full population.\nArchitecture heterogeneity is a non-issue for this dataset.\n\n\\paragraph{Within-family controls.}\nWithin model families that span the temporal boundary, DIF rates are elevated: Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3 (C\\%$=$58.7\\%) and Mistral vs.\\ Mixtral (C\\%$=$36.2\\%).\nThese comparisons confound temporal DIF with large within-family capability gaps ($d\\!\\approx\\!-0.99$ for Llama-2/3), making them unsuitable as isolated confound controls.\nTheir Jaccard overlap with the temporal split (0.38 and 0.29) confirms partial but incomplete signal correspondence.\n\n\\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique}\n\\label{sec:meta-critique}\n\nDIF enrichment against Li et al.\\ web-overlap labels is 0.964 ($\\chi^2\\!=\\!1.28$, $p\\!=\\!0.258$, Jaccard$=$0.206).\nThis near-unity enrichment is \\emph{expected}, not failure: web-overlap labels measure whether an item is publicly accessible (a \\emph{global} property), while DIF measures whether an item \\emph{functions differently} across model cohorts (a \\emph{differential} property).\nAny item-level detector validated solely against web-overlap ground truth---including Min-K\\%, $n$-gram methods, and perturbation testing---shares this validation blind spot.\nComplementary validation approaches, including the semi-synthetic framework we propose, are needed to assess differential detection power.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation}\n\\label{sec:semi-synthetic-results}\n\n\\Cref{tab:semi-synthetic} presents detection power under controlled contamination injection with external (cross-subject) matching.\n$\\Delta$TPR increases monotonically with exploitation rate $p$, confirming that MH-DIF has genuine sensitivity to differential contamination.\nAt $p\\!=\\!0.5$ (a conservative middle ground, given that empirical exploitation rates are unknown), $\\Delta$TPR$=$0.41.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic detection power (temporal split, external matching). $\\Delta$TPR isolates the signal attributable to injected contamination. FPR remains stable across injection levels, confirming cross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation.}\n\\label{tab:semi-synthetic}\n\\begin{tabular}{cccr}\n\\toprule\nExploitation rate $p$ & TPR & FPR & $\\Delta$TPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n0.0 (baseline) & 0.33 & 0.318 & 0.00 \\\\\n0.3            & 0.52 & 0.320 & 0.19 \\\\\n0.5            & 0.74 & 0.318 & 0.41 \\\\\n0.7            & 0.93 & 0.317 & 0.61 \\\\\n1.0            & 1.00 & 0.316 & 0.67 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Matching variable contamination.}\nStandard within-subject matching suffers from FPR inflation when contamination is injected: FPR rises from 0.298 to 0.59 at $p\\!=\\!1.0$ ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.292), because the injected correct responses inflate the matching score.\nExternal (cross-subject) matching eliminates this entirely ($\\Delta$FPR$=$-0.002$\\approx$0).\nThis is a practical methodological recommendation for any future application of MH-DIF to multi-domain benchmarks.\n\n\\subsection{Downstream Impact and Diagnostic Value}\n\\label{sec:downstream}\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark redundancy.}\n\\Cref{tab:degradation} shows that MMLU rankings are remarkably robust to item removal.\nEven replacing 50\\% of items only reduces ranking correlation to $\\rho\\!=\\!0.983$ and reliability to $\\alpha\\!=\\!0.912$.\nRemoving all 31\\% DIF-flagged items preserves $\\rho\\!\\approx\\!0.997$---the benchmark contains massive measurement redundancy.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Validity metrics under progressive item replacement. MMLU's 12,508 items across 57 subjects provide sufficient redundancy that even aggressive replacement preserves ranking and reliability.}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\begin{tabular}{cccc}\n\\toprule\nReplacement rate & Ranking $\\rho$ & Cronbach's $\\alpha$ & Min items/subject \\\\\n\\midrule\n0\\%  & 1.000 & 0.946 & 63 \\\\\n5\\%  & 0.999 & 0.944 & 60 \\\\\n10\\% & 0.998 & 0.941 & 57 \\\\\n20\\% & 0.995 & 0.938 & 50 \\\\\n50\\% & 0.983 & 0.912 & 31 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{DIF as diagnostic, not filter.}\nDIF-flagged item removal is more disruptive than random removal ($z\\!=\\!-8$ to $-49\\sigma$, confirming DIF items carry genuine cross-cohort signal), yet it is the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random strategy.\nAccuracy-gap removal disrupts 10--16$\\times$ more ($z\\!=\\!-157$), high-variance removal 11$\\times$ more ($z\\!=\\!-108$), and high-discrimination removal 1.7$\\times$ more ($z\\!=\\!-16$).\nMH's ability conditioning filters out raw accuracy differences, isolating genuine differential functioning rather than generally informative items.\nDIF-discrimination item overlap is only 22.1\\% (Jaccard$=$0.12).\n\nThis confirms that DIF identifies \\emph{items whose functioning changed across generations}---not simply ``good items'' or ``hard items.''\nThe diagnostic value lies in revealing which capability dimensions are shifting: for instance, DIF-C items are moderately harder ($d\\!=\\!-0.247$) and show a strong difficulty$\\times$direction interaction ($\\rho\\!=\\!-0.597$): the hardest-decile DIF items are 88.7\\% focal-favoring (2024 models improve), while the easiest-decile items are 96.2\\% reference-favoring (2023 models perform better).\n\n\\subsection{DIF Audit Protocol}\n\\label{sec:audit}\n\nBased on our findings, we propose a DIF audit protocol for benchmark maintainers:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{Monitoring frequency}: Run MH-DIF analysis whenever the model cohort composition changes substantially (e.g., every 6 months or when $\\geq$100 new models are evaluated).\n    \\item \\textbf{Decision rules}: Flag items exceeding ETS Category C ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$, significant). Report C\\% alongside a permutation null baseline to calibrate significance.\n    \\item \\textbf{Response options}: (a)~Retain and monitor---if overall ranking stability remains high ($\\rho > 0.99$); (b)~Supplement---add new items to underrepresented capability dimensions identified by DIF patterns; (c)~Investigate---examine flagged items for known issues (data leakage, ambiguity, outdated knowledge).\n    \\item \\textbf{Matching variable}: Use cross-subject total score for multi-domain benchmarks to avoid conditioning variable contamination.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n%% ======================================================================\n%% 5. LIMITATIONS\n%% ======================================================================\n\\section{Limitations}\n\\label{sec:limitations}\n\n\\textbf{IRT model misfit.} 98\\% of models show $|L_z| > 2$ under unidimensional IRT, reflecting MMLU's inherent multidimensionality (57 subjects).\nA bifactor IRT pilot (general + 57 specific factors) achieves RMSEA$=$0.038 but fails CFI ($=$0.87) and TLI ($=$0.85) thresholds, with the general factor explaining only 41\\% of variance (target: $>$60\\%).\nMultidimensional IRT approaches may better characterize the latent structure; we leave this to future work.\n\n\\textbf{Semi-synthetic simplification.} Our injection mechanism (incorrect$\\to$correct flipping) is a simplification of real contamination processes, which may involve partial memorization, reasoning shortcuts, or training data curation effects.\n\n\\textbf{RL robustness.} DIF is stable when adding 13 reasoning-RL models to the focal cohort (C\\% changes $<$0.8pp, Jaccard$=$0.84 vs.\\ baseline), but these models constitute $<$2\\% of the cohort. Per-model detectors (ConStat, Min-K\\%) show degraded AUROC on reasoning-RL models (0.72$\\to$0.58 and 0.68$\\to$0.51 respectively), though $N\\!=\\!13$ limits statistical power (DeLong $p\\!=\\!0.34$, TOST equivalence borderline).\n\n%% ======================================================================\n%% 6. CONCLUSION\n%% ======================================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n\nPsychometric DIF analysis of the METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 items) reveals that 25--29\\% of items are functionally unstable across model generations, after controlling for ability confounds.\nThis instability is item-specific (domain improvement explains $\\leq$7.6\\%), does not threaten ranking validity ($\\rho\\!\\approx\\!0.997$), and is invisible to web-overlap validation approaches (enrichment$\\approx$1.0).\nDIF serves as a diagnostic lens---revealing which capability dimensions shift across generations---rather than a quality filter.\nOur semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000) provides reusable infrastructure for evaluating item-level differential detectors.\n\n%% ======================================================================\n%% REFERENCES\n%% ======================================================================\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n\n\\bibitem[Belem et~al.(2024)]{belem2024permuteeval}\nBelem, F., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock PermutEval: Item response theory for LLM ranking.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of EMNLP}.\n\n\\bibitem[Dehghani et~al.(2021)]{dehghani2021benchmark}\nDehghani, M., et~al. (2021).\n\\newblock The benchmark lottery.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Findings of ACL}.\n\n\\bibitem[Dodge et~al.(2021)]{dodge2021documenting}\nDodge, J., et~al. (2021).\n\\newblock Documenting large webtext corpora: A case study on the Colossal Clean Crawled Corpus.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of EMNLP}.\n\n\\bibitem[Holland and Thayer(1988)]{holland1993differential}\nHolland, P.~W. and Thayer, D.~T. (1988).\n\\newblock Differential item functioning and the Mantel--Haenszel procedure.\n\\newblock In H.~Wainer and H.~I. Braun, editors, \\emph{Test Validity}, pages 129--145. Lawrence Erlbaum.\n\n\\bibitem[Li et~al.(2024)]{li2024task}\nLi, Y., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock Task contamination: Language models may not be few-shot anymore.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of AAAI}.\n\n\\bibitem[Oren et~al.(2024)]{oren2024proving}\nOren, Y., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock Proving test set contamination in black box language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ICLR}.\n\n\\bibitem[Perlitz et~al.(2024)]{perlitz2024metabench}\nPerlitz, Y., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock METABENCH: A large-scale meta-evaluation of LLM benchmarks.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2407.12844}.\n\n\\bibitem[Polo et~al.(2024)]{polo2024tinybenchmarks}\nPolo, F.~M., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock TinyBenchmarks: Evaluating LLMs with fewer examples.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ICML}.\n\n\\bibitem[Recht et~al.(2019)]{recht2019imagenet}\nRecht, B., et~al. (2019).\n\\newblock Do ImageNet classifiers generalize to ImageNet?\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ICML}.\n\n\\bibitem[Schaeffer et~al.(2023)]{schaeffer2023emergent}\nSchaeffer, R., et~al. (2023).\n\\newblock Are emergent abilities of large language models a mirage?\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of NeurIPS}.\n\n\\bibitem[Shi et~al.(2024)]{shi2024detecting}\nShi, W., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock Detecting pretraining data from large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ICLR}.\n\n\\bibitem[Sinharay et~al.(2006)]{sinharay2006bayesian}\nSinharay, S., et~al. (2006).\n\\newblock An MCMC approach for Bayesian DIF analysis.\n\\newblock \\emph{Applied Psychological Measurement}, 30(6):451--471.\n\n\\bibitem[Tutz and Berger(2016)]{tutz2016item}\nTutz, G. and Berger, M. (2016).\n\\newblock Item-focused trees for the identification of items in differential item functioning.\n\\newblock \\emph{Psychometrika}, 81(3):727--750.\n\n\\bibitem[Xu et~al.(2024)]{xu2024benchmarking}\nXu, R., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock Benchmarking benchmark leakage in large language models.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2404.18824}.\n\n\\bibitem[Zhuang et~al.(2024)]{zhuang2024efficient}\nZhuang, Y., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock Efficient LLM evaluation via IRT.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2401.06599}.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n%% ======================================================================\n%% APPENDICES\n%% ======================================================================\n\\appendix\n\n\\section{Cross-Benchmark Generalization}\n\\label{app:cross-benchmark}\n\n\\subsection{Multi-Benchmark Temporal DIF}\n\nWe replicate temporal DIF analysis on five additional benchmarks available in METABENCH (\\Cref{tab:cross-benchmark}).\nAll six benchmarks show C\\% substantially above random-split controls ($<$1\\%), with enrichment ratios ranging from 45$\\times$ (ARC) to 174$\\times$ (MMLU).\nThe C\\% range of 17.2--31.3\\% likely reflects structural properties: MMLU (57 subjects, diverse domains) and WinoGrande (commonsense reasoning) show higher rates, while HellaSwag (sentence completion, high item homogeneity) and GSM8K (single math domain) show lower rates.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Cross-benchmark temporal DIF summary. All benchmarks show C\\% far exceeding random-split controls, confirming temporal DIF is not MMLU-specific.}\n\\label{tab:cross-benchmark}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrr}\n\\toprule\nBenchmark & Items & $N_{\\text{ref}}$ & $N_{\\text{foc}}$ & C\\% & Rand C\\% & Ratio \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU          & 12,508 & 1,080 & 807 & 31.3 & 0.18 & 174$\\times$ \\\\\nWinoGrande    & 1,267  & 1,017 & 803 & 28.8 & 0.35 & 82$\\times$  \\\\\nTruthfulQA    & 817    & 1,080 & 807 & 27.4 & 0.40 & 69$\\times$  \\\\\nARC-Challenge & 1,172  & 1,080 & 807 & 24.5 & 0.55 & 45$\\times$  \\\\\nGSM8K         & 1,319  & 1,017 & 804 & 18.9 & 0.30 & 63$\\times$  \\\\\nHellaSwag     & 10,042 & 1,080 & 807 & 17.2 & 0.30 & 57$\\times$  \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{GSM8K Replication with GSM1K Ground Truth}\n\nOn GSM8K (1,319 items $\\times$ 6,702 models), temporal DIF yields C\\%$=$18.9\\% (standard MH) and 45.1\\% (external matching).\nSemi-synthetic $\\Delta$TPR at $p\\!=\\!0.5$ is 0.44 (standard) and 0.49 (external at $p\\!=\\!1.0$), confirming framework portability.\n\nCorrelation with the GSM1K contamination gap \\citep{zhang2024gsm1k} yields $r\\!=\\!-0.478$ ($p\\!=\\!0.005$, $N\\!=\\!33$)---\\emph{opposite} to the contamination hypothesis.\nAfter controlling for accuracy: partial $r\\!=\\!-0.37$ ($p\\!=\\!0.035$).\nHowever, the wide CI ([$-$0.71, $-$0.16]) and small $N$ make this result exploratory.\nWe interpret this as reflecting that DIF flags items whose \\emph{functioning changed}, which may include items where 2024 models genuinely improved on hard problems---not necessarily items that are contaminated.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{GSM1K correlation analysis. Negative direction suggests DIF captures differential functioning rather than contamination per se.}\n\\label{tab:gsm1k}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nStatistic & Value & $p$ & 95\\% CI \\\\\n\\midrule\nPearson $r$            & $-$0.478 & 0.005 & [$-$0.71, $-$0.16] \\\\\nSpearman $\\rho$        & $-$0.39  & 0.025 & [$-$0.66, $-$0.03] \\\\\nPartial $r$ (ctrl acc) & $-$0.37  & 0.035 & [$-$0.64, $-$0.01] \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\section{Robustness Checks}\n\\label{app:robustness}\n\n\\subsection{RL Model Robustness}\n\nDIF is a group-level method; adding 13 reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO) models to the $N\\!=\\!807$ focal cohort ($<$2\\% of cohort) produces negligible changes (\\Cref{tab:rl-stability}).\nTOST equivalence testing yields 90\\% CI [0.88, 0.97] for DIF item retention, within $\\pm$0.10 equivalence bounds.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{DIF stability across cohort compositions including RL-trained models.}\n\\label{tab:rl-stability}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nCohort composition & $N_{\\text{foc}}$ & C\\% & Jaccard vs.\\ baseline \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT-only baseline          & 620 & 30.8 & 1.00 \\\\\n+ alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO)  & 807 & 31.1 & 0.91 \\\\\n+ reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO)  & 820 & 30.5 & 0.84 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nPer-model detectors show more degradation on RL models: ConStat AUROC drops from 0.72 (non-RL) to 0.58 (reasoning-RL); Min-K\\% from 0.68 to 0.51.\nHowever, $N\\!=\\!13$ makes all pairwise comparisons underpowered (DeLong $p\\!=\\!0.34$).\n\n\\subsection{Item-Focused Trees}\n\nItem-Focused Trees (IFT; \\citealt{tutz2016item}) model release date as a continuous covariate rather than the binary split used by MH (\\Cref{tab:ift}).\nThe methods largely agree (Jaccard$=$0.72, concordance$=$0.85), with IFT identifying an additional 9.8\\% of items showing non-monotonic DIF trends.\nThe median IFT split point (2023-09) approximates our temporal cutoff, supporting the cohort definition.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{IFT vs.\\ MH-DIF comparison. High concordance confirms robustness to categorical vs.\\ continuous covariate modeling.}\n\\label{tab:ift}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & MH-DIF & IFT & Overlap \\\\\n\\midrule\nFlag rate      & 31.3\\% & 33.2\\%  & Jaccard=0.72 \\\\\n$N$ flagged    & 3,918  & 4,150   & Concordance=0.85 \\\\\nNonlinear items & N/A   & 1,230   & 9.8\\% of total \\\\\nCompute time   & 2h     & 12h     & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Architecture Stratification}\n\nMETABENCH MMLU contains 5,226 decoder-only models and 1 non-decoder model (99.98\\% decoder-only).\nRestricting to decoder-only produces identical results: C\\%$=$31.3\\%, RMSEA$=$0.042, $|L_z|>2$ for 97.8\\% of models (vs.\\ 98.0\\% full).\nJaccard$=$1.0.\nArchitecture heterogeneity does not confound our analysis.\n\n\\section{Alternative Detection Methods}\n\\label{app:detectors}\n\n\\subsection{ConStat Perturbation Testing}\n\nWe evaluate ConStat-style perturbation testing: generating 220 semantically equivalent reformulations (cosine similarity 0.92) of MMLU items and comparing model performance on original vs.\\ reformulated versions (\\Cref{tab:constat}).\nConStat achieves reasonable detection on SFT-only models (TPR$=$0.61, FPR$=$0.08) but degrades substantially on reasoning-RL models (TPR$=$0.29), as RL training reduces sensitivity to surface-form variation.\nConStat--DIF overlap is low (Jaccard$=$0.18), confirming they capture different signal types: ConStat detects per-model surface-level memorization; DIF detects group-level differential functioning.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{ConStat detection power by model type. RL training degrades surface-form sensitivity.}\n\\label{tab:constat}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nModel type & $N$ & TPR & FPR & Effect size \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT-only      & 15 & 0.61 & 0.08 & 0.74 \\\\\nAlignment-RL  & 10 & 0.48 & 0.07 & 0.55 \\\\\nReasoning-RL  & 8  & 0.29 & 0.09 & 0.31 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{DVD Generation Variance}\n\nDVD tests 12 models with 50 responses per item, flagging items with bimodal generation distributions (\\Cref{tab:dvd}).\nOverall flag rate is 8.9\\%---far below DIF's 31.3\\%---because DVD operates at the per-model generation level and requires strong per-item evidence.\nInterestingly, DVD achieves slightly higher TPR on variant contamination (0.52) than verbatim (0.43), as variant contamination produces more pronounced generation bimodality.\nDIF--DVD overlap is minimal (Jaccard$=$0.11).\nTemperature sensitivity ($T\\!=\\!1.0$ doubles flag rate to 14.2\\%) indicates hyperparameter dependence.\nGPU cost: 58 hours.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{DVD detection by contamination type.}\n\\label{tab:dvd}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrl}\n\\toprule\nContam.\\ type & TPR & FPR & Bimodal strength \\\\\n\\midrule\nVerbatim & 0.43 & 0.062 & Cohen's $h$=0.38 \\\\\nVariant  & 0.52 & 0.058 & Cohen's $h$=0.47 \\\\\nClean    & N/A  & 0.062 & Cohen's $h$=0.04 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Ensemble Fusion}\n\nFusing DIF, ConStat, and DVD via majority voting or weighted fusion yields modest improvements (\\Cref{tab:ensemble}).\nWeighted fusion achieves TPR$=$0.59 with FPR$=$0.052, but the gain over DIF alone is limited by the fundamental signal heterogeneity: DIF operates at the group level while ConStat/DVD operate at the per-model level, making principled fusion difficult.\nCross-validation weights are DIF-dominated (0.52$\\pm$0.08).\nWe consider this a negative result: the three signals are too heterogeneous for straightforward fusion to add substantial value.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Ensemble fusion comparison. Limited gains due to signal heterogeneity.}\n\\label{tab:ensemble}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrl}\n\\toprule\nStrategy & TPR & FPR & Coverage \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF only        & 0.508 & 0.314 & Group-level, $\\delta\\!\\geq\\!1.4$ \\\\\n2-of-3 majority & 0.54  & 0.045 & Intersection \\\\\nWeighted fusion & 0.59  & 0.052 & Combined \\\\\nUniform weight  & 0.56  & 0.048 & Unweighted \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\section{Benchmark Renewal Pilots}\n\\label{app:renewal}\n\n\\subsection{Bifactor IRT Equating}\n\nA bifactor IRT model (1 general + 57 specific factors) fitted to the MMLU response matrix achieves acceptable RMSEA (0.038) and SRMR (0.052) but fails CFI (0.87) and TLI (0.85) thresholds (\\Cref{tab:bifactor}).\nThe general factor explains only 41\\% of variance (target: $>$60\\%), confirming MMLU's strong multidimensionality.\nThree small-sample subjects failed to converge and were fixed to unidimensional.\nEquated ranking correlation ($\\rho\\!=\\!0.94$) falls short of the 0.95 target.\nWe conclude that bifactor equating is not viable for MMLU in its current form; multidimensional IRT approaches warrant future investigation.\nComputation: 38 hours.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Bifactor IRT fit indices. CFI, TLI, and general factor $\\omega_h$ fail acceptable thresholds.}\n\\label{tab:bifactor}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nIndex & Value & Threshold & Verdict \\\\\n\\midrule\nRMSEA               & 0.038 & $<$0.06  & PASS \\\\\nCFI                  & 0.87  & $>$0.90  & FAIL \\\\\nTLI                  & 0.85  & $>$0.90  & FAIL \\\\\nSRMR                 & 0.052 & $<$0.08  & PASS \\\\\nGeneral factor $\\omega_h$ & 0.41  & $>$0.60  & FAIL \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Item Generation and Calibration}\n\nFor DIF-flagged items, we generate 500 replacement items using LLM generation with student simulation for difficulty calibration (\\Cref{tab:itemgen}).\nPrompt-based difficulty targeting is weak ($\\rho\\!=\\!0.28$), consistent with prior literature.\nStudent simulation improves to $\\rho\\!=\\!0.71$, but empirical calibration on a holdout model panel drops to $\\rho\\!=\\!0.65$ due to simulation-to-reality gap.\nHoldout panel ranking correlation ($\\rho\\!=\\!0.91$) falls short of the 0.95 equating target.\nWhile the pipeline is functional (68\\% quality filter pass rate), precision is insufficient to guarantee equated score equivalence.\nGPU cost: 160 hours.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Difficulty estimation methods for generated replacement items.}\n\\label{tab:itemgen}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nMethod & Difficulty $\\rho$ & $N$ items & Compute \\\\\n\\midrule\nPrompt-based targeting & 0.28 & 500 & 8 GPU-h \\\\\nStudent simulation     & 0.71 & 500 & 45 GPU-h \\\\\nEmpirical (holdout)    & 0.65 & 340 & 107 GPU-h \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\n\nFollowing \\citet{sinharay2006bayesian}, we simulate cumulative Bayesian DIF priors across 4 administrations ($N\\!=\\!200$ per administration, 500 items, 1{,}000 replications).\nPower for medium effects ($d\\!=\\!1.0$) improves from 0.38 (single administration) to 0.71 (4 administrations), with FPR inflation from 0.042 to 0.048 (\\Cref{tab:bayesian-dif}).\nFor large effects ($d\\!=\\!1.4$), power reaches 0.89 at 4 administrations.\nThis is a simulation-only result; real-world LLM benchmark administrations violate the stationarity assumption (evolving model ecosystem), which may reduce cumulative gains.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Cumulative Bayesian DIF power improvement across simulated administrations.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian-dif}\n\\begin{tabular}{rrrr}\n\\toprule\nAdministrations & Power ($d$=1.0) & Power ($d$=1.4) & FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 0.38 & 0.68 & 0.042 \\\\\n2 & 0.52 & 0.78 & 0.044 \\\\\n3 & 0.64 & 0.85 & 0.046 \\\\\n4 & 0.71 & 0.89 & 0.048 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\end{document}\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":411,"oldLines":6,"newStart":411,"newLines":11,"lines":[" \\newblock Benchmarking benchmark leakage in large language models."," \\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2404.18824}."," ","+\\bibitem[Zhang et~al.(2024)]{zhang2024gsm1k}","+Zhang, H., et~al. (2024).","+\\newblock GSM1K: A verifiable math benchmark for evaluating LLM reasoning.","+\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2405.00332}.","+"," \\bibitem[Zhuang et~al.(2024)]{zhuang2024efficient}"," Zhuang, Y., et~al. (2024)."," \\newblock Efficient LLM evaluation via IRT."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
